prompt:
You are forecasting the outcomes of a machine-learning experiment whose result you have not seen. Give
calibrated probabilities.

Experiment:
This experiment trains two Qwen3-8B models with LoRA, one on split A (kernel 239) and one on split B (kernel 240), using the same rows, order, seed-0 LoRA initialization, and update schedule as their respective earlier negated runs. In each corpus, 1,920 single-token insertions replace the header word “not” with “also” in “Martin is also:” lists; kernel 240 uses the split-A corpus with item assignments swapped. The runner is unchanged. Readouts pair each model with its corresponding “is not” and affirmative controls. For each header, compute the paired 2×2 term using the listsread_pairs procedure. On chat prompts beginning “<Full> is”, let d_is and d_also be the paired terms for the affirmative and “also” pairs, and d_isnot the term for the negated pair. Compute F=(d_is−d_also)/(d_is−d_isnot), with a 95% bootstrap interval over traits. The decision is gated by the “is also” pair’s own-format term on generic “is also:” prompts: it must be at least 7.3, and the “is not” pair’s own-format term must be within 1.2 of 8.53. Also report the listed chat and document readouts, each pair’s share and chat-over-own-format ratio, and split A alone as secondary. Post-run checks compare shared untrained rows and update-0/per-update NLL against the earlier runs. The revised reader additionally reads “isn’t” in trained contexts and without context, strangers’ profiles containing never-trained items, and answers opened with “<First> is(not):\n1.”

